23135.jpg" style="display: block; padding: 1em 0; text-align: center; "><img alt="" border="0" width="320" data-original-height="720" data-original-width="1280" src="https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEhhsAc29WORgQvSotpIcfic1Jbd5v7J7cksAbMMXs5RA0bnSu7Rp0kEFxteuS-uazgcmfdGpAa3SoUU0DiXXtU6_-WOgKTADYWSIcg1_JQ2GtE4Pb4vURPqMQ_r4uFQBFGnMiyMH_Hm89ihqvauIzm6sXG1vFZAreONVJ0gcisZptyhKFiRJgirGDAFX5Sq/s320/1000023135.jpg"/></a></div>
    <header>

        

        <p>Free up space, boost performance, and declutter your PC with these proven methods.</p>

    </header>

    

    <main>

        <article>

            <section>

                <h2>Why Uninstall System Apps on Windows 11?</h2>

                <p>Windows 11 comes packed with pre-installed system apps and bloatware that can slow down your device, consume storage, and clutter your Start menu.<grok:render card_id="19817e" card_type="citation_card" type="render_inline_citation">

<argument name="citation_id">17</argument>

</grok:render> Removing them not only frees up space but also improves overall performance. In this 2025 guide, we''ll cover built-in methods, advanced PowerShell techniques, and the best tools for safe removal.</p>

                

                <div id="toc">

                    <h3>Table of Contents</h3>

                    <ul>

                        <li><a href="#built-in">Using Built-in Settings</a></li>

                        <li><a href="#powershell">PowerShell Method</a></li>

                        <li><a href="#tools">Best Tools for Bloatware Removal</a></li>

                        <li><a href="#tips">Pro Tips & Warnings</a></li>

                    </ul>

                </div>

            </section>

            

            <section id="built-in">

                <h2>Step-by-Step: Uninstall System Apps Using Built-in Settings</h2>

                <p>This is the simplest way to remove most apps without third-party software.<grok:render card_id="15d5ad" card_type="citation_card" type="render_inline_citation">

<argument name="citation_id">2</argument>

</grok:render> Follow these steps:</p>

                

                <div class="collapsible">Method 1: Via Settings App</div>

                <div class="content">

                    <ol class="step">

                        <li>Press <kbd>Windows + I</kbd> to open Settings.</li>

                        <li>Navigate to <strong>Apps > Installed apps</strong>.</li>

                        <li>Scroll through the list or use the search bar to find the system app (e.g., "Xbox" or "Candy Crush").</li>

                        <li>Click the three dots (...) next to the app and select <strong>Uninstall</strong>.</li>

                        <li>Confirm the removal and restart your PC if prompted.</li>

                    </ol>

                </div>

                

                <div class="collapsible">Method 2: Via Start Menu</div>

                <div class="content">

                    <ol class="step">

                        <li>Right-click the app tile in the Start menu.</li>

                        <li>Select <strong>Uninstall</strong> from the context menu.</li>

                        <li>Follow the on-screen prompts to complete.</li>

                    </ol>

                </div>

                

                <p>Note: Some protected system apps (like Microsoft Edge) can''t be removed this way.<grok:render card_id="29c981" card_type="citation_card" type="render_inline_citation">

<argument name="citation_id">13</argument>

</grok:render></p>

            </section>

            

            <section id="powershell">

                <h2>Advanced: Remove Built-in Apps with PowerShell</h2>

                <p>For stubborn bloatware, PowerShell offers precise control.<grok:render card_id="bf6519" card_type="citation_card" type="render_inline_citation">

<argument name="citation_id">12</argument>

</grok:render> Run as Administrator:</p>

                

                <div class="collapsible">Step-by-Step PowerShell Commands</div>

                <div class="content">

                    <ol class="step">

                        <li>Right-click Start > <strong>Terminal (Admin)</strong> or search for PowerShell and run as admin.</li>

                        <li>List all apps: <code>Get-AppxPackage | Select Name, PackageFullName</code></li>

                        <li>Remove a specific app: <code>Get-AppxPackage *AppName* | Remove-AppxPackage</code> (e.g., <code>Get-AppxPackage *xbox* | Remove-AppxPackage</code>).</li>

                        <li>For all users: <code>Get-AppxProvisionedPackage -Online | Where-Object {$_.DisplayName -like "*AppName*"} | Remove-AppxProvisionedPackage -Online</code>.</li>

                        <li>Restart your PC.</li>

                    </ol>

                    <p>Warning: Be cautious—removing essential apps can cause issues.</p>

                </div>

            </section>

            

            <section id="tools">

                <h2>Best Tools to Remove Bloatware on Windows 11 (2025 Edition)</h2>

                <p>If manual methods aren''t enough, these tools automate the process.<grok:render card_id="2d77b1" card_type="citation_card" type="render_inline_citation">

<argument name="citation_id">15</argument>

</grok:render><grok:render card_id="f04167" card_type="citation_card" type="render_inline_citation">

<argument name="citation_id">16</argument>

</grok:render></p>

                

                <div class="tool">

                    <h3>1. CCleaner (Free)</h3>

                    <p>Intuitive for batch removal.<grok:render card_id="8e8577" card_type="citation_card" type="render_inline_citation">

<argument name="citation_id">11</argument>

</grok:render></p>

                    <ul>

                        <li>Download from official site.</li>

                        <li>Go to Tools > Uninstall > Select apps > Uninstall.</li>

                    </ul>

                </div>

                

                <div class="tool">

                    <h3>2. Talon (Open-Source, New in 2025)</h3>

                    <p>Debloat in 2 clicks—ideal for quick cleanups.<grok:render card_id="459e0d" card_type="citation_card" type="render_inline_citation">

<argument name="citation_id">16</argument>

</grok:render></p>

                    <ul>

                        <li>Download from GitHub.</li>

                        <li>Run the tool and select presets for bloatware removal.</li>

                    </ul>

                </div>

                

                <div class="tool">

                    <h3>3. Win11Debloat (PowerShell Script)</h3>

                    <p>Lightweight script for full decluttering.<grok:render card_id="60dc1d" card_type="citation_card" type="render_inline_citation">

<argument name="citation_id">12</argument>

</grok:render></p>

                    <ul>

